## 7. Cost Model & CBO

The cost-based optimizer (CBO) is the second phase. It
takes the equivalence classes produced by the RBO and
selects a *physical plan* — a tree of physical operators —
under a cost model.

### 7.1 Physical operator inventory

The physical operators are the *implementations* of the
logical operators. Each physical operator carries
parameters that the CBO can tune.

| Physical | Implements                        | Notes                                 |
|----------|-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------|
| `H_{V,q,τ}`   | `σ_{‖V−q‖<τ}`                 | ANN range scan; strategy ∈ {HNSW, IVF, scan}; HNSW params `ef, M`; IVF params `nlist, nprobe`; scan cost linear in `|R|`.|
| `B_{col,r}`   | `σ_{col ∈ r}`                  | B-tree range scan.                    |
| `F(R)`        | base scan                        | Sequential file scan.                 |
| `I_{t,q,θ}`   | `σ_{BM25>θ}`                   | Inverted index probe.                 |
| `G_{g,d,dir}` | `Γ_{g,d}`                      | Graph traversal; `dir` is direction.  |
| `Sort(O)`     | `τ_O`                           | Full sort.                            |
| `TopK(O,K)`   | `τ_O Λ_K` (T1)                 | Bounded-heap top-K.                   |
| `Materialize(e→c)` | `μ_{e→c}`                  | Computes `e` and binds `c`.           |
| `HashJoin`, `NLJ`, `MergeJoin` | `⋈_ψ`             | Standard join algorithms.             |
| `Partition`, `HashBuild`, `HashProbe` | hash-based operators | Standard enforcers.        |

### 7.2 Cost dimensions

The cost model has four dimensions. The first three are
classical; the fourth is new.

- **I/O cost.** Page reads and writes. Dominant for
  cold data; mitigated by indexes.
- **CPU cost.** Distance computations, BM25 scoring,
  tuple materialization. For vector queries, the
  per-probe distance computation is the dominant
  component.
- **Memory cost.** Working set size, in particular the
  size of intermediate hash tables and the priority
  queue for `TopK`.
- **Approximation cost.** The *quality penalty* of
  choosing an approximate physical operator over an
  exact one. For the ANN family this is expressed as
  `1 − recall(strategy, params)`: HNSW depends on
  `ef, M`; IVF on `nlist, nprobe`; the scan strategy
  is exact (recall = 1) but its cost is `O(|R|·d)`. The
  `topK` physical operator is exact given a correct
  scoring function (approximation = 0). The CBO
  expresses approximation as a constraint — "the chosen
  plan must have recall ≥ `ρ_min`" — or as a term in the
  cost function weighted by application preference.

The cost model is *additive across operators in a
pipeline*, weighted by estimated cardinalities at each
boundary. The estimation primitives (cardinality,
distinct-value count, range selectivity) are themselves
non-trivial in the multi-modal setting; we discuss
their realization in the prototype (Section 8).

### 7.3 Logical-to-physical bridge

For each logical operator, the CBO enumerates a set of
candidate physical realizations. The bridge is a set of
*implementation rules* in the Cascades sense:

- `σ_{‖V−q‖<τ}(R)` may be realized as `H_{V,q,τ}(R)`
  with one of three strategies: HNSW (if an HNSW index
  exists on `V`), IVF (if an IVF index exists), or a
  brute-force scan over `μ_{‖V−q‖→d} F(R)` followed by
  `σ_{d<τ}`. The CBO also considers the *hybrid* plan:
  ANN with conservative parameters to produce
  candidates, followed by an exact re-evaluation of the
  threshold. When the modality mix is unknown a priori
  (e.g. before cost calibration), the scan baseline is
  always available and provides a useful ceiling.
- `σ_{BM25>θ}(R)` may be realized as `I_{t,q,θ}(R)`
  if an inverted index exists on `t`; otherwise as a
  full scan with BM25 scoring.
- `τ_O Λ_K(R)` is realized as `TopK(O, K)(R)`, per
  T1. The CBO may also consider `Sort(O)(R)` followed
  by `Limit(K)`, useful when the cost of sorting is
  amortized over a downstream group-by.

### 7.4 Adaptive execution

Static cost estimates are notoriously wrong, especially
for vector workloads where selectivity is hard to
predict from small samples. The prototype is designed
to support **adaptive execution**: at well-defined
plan points (e.g. after an ANN probe returns its
candidate set), the executor re-consults the cost
model with observed cardinalities and may switch to a
different sub-plan. The algebra is unchanged; only the
physical subtree is replaced. This is a feature
enabled by the logical/physical separation: an
adaptive switch is *safe* by construction, because the
two physical subtrees are both implementations of the
same logical operator.

### 7.5 Formal cost model and CBO complexity

The cost model of 7.2 is a *vector* of four components,
each of which can be reasoned about symbolically. This
subsection makes the cost model formal and analyzes the
complexity of the CBO's search.

**Cost as a 4-tuple.** For a logical plan `P`, define

$$
\mathrm{Cost}(P) \;=\; (\mathrm{io}(P),\; \mathrm{cpu}(P),\; \mathrm{mem}(P),\; \mathrm{approx}(P)) \;\in\; \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^4.
$$

The CBO's objective is to choose, among all physical
realizations of the canonical form of `P`, the one that
*minimizes a weighted sum* of the four components (with
weights set by application policy), subject to a
*constraint* `approx(P) ≤ ε` for an application-chosen
`ε ∈ [0, 1]`. Equivalently, the CBO can minimize a
Pareto-front: a plan `P₁` Pareto-dominates `P₂` if
`Cost(P₁) ≤ Cost(P₂)` componentwise and at least one
component is strictly smaller.

**Per-operator cost expressions.** Each physical
operator has a symbolic cost expression. We give the
expressions for the core physical operators; the cost
of a plan is the sum of the costs of its operators,
adjusted by the cardinality at each operator's input.

| Physical operator | Cost vector |
|---|---|
| `F(R)` (file scan) | `(B(R), 0, B(R), 0)` |
| `B_{col, range}(R)` (B-tree range) | `(O(log N + k), 0, k, 0)` |
| `H_{V, q, τ}(R)` (HNSW range) | `(O(ef · log N), O(ef · d), O(ef), 1 − recall(ef, M))` |
| `H_{V, q, τ}(R)` (IVF range) | `(O(nprobe · d), O(nprobe · d · d_quant), O(nprobe), 1 − recall(nprobe, nlist))` |
| `H_{V, q, τ}(R)` (brute-force scan) | `(O(B(R)), O(|R| · d), O(1), 0)` |
| `I_{col, term}` (inverted index) | `(O(postings_size), O(postings_size · score), O(postings_size), 0)` |
| `G_{g, d}` (graph traversal) | `(O(|V| + |E|) bounded by depth d, O(visited), O(visited), 0)` |
| `Sort(O)(R)` | `(O(B log B), O(B log B), O(B), 0)` |
| `TopK(O, K)(R)` | `(O(B log K), O(B log K), O(K), 0)` |
| `Materialize(e→c)(R)` | `(0, O(|R| · cost(e)), O(|R|), 0)` |
| `HashJoin(R, S)` | `(B(R) + B(S), O(|R| + |S|), O(min(\|R\|, \|S\|)), 0)` |
| `NLJ(R, S)` | `(B(R) · \|S\|, O(\|R\| · \|S\|), 0, 0)` |

In the table, `B(R)` denotes the page count of `R`,
`N` the cardinality, `k` the result size, `d` the
embedding dimensionality, `ef` and `M` the HNSW
parameters, `nlist` and `nprobe` the IVF parameters,
`d_quant` the per-probe quantized-distance cost, and
`|·|` cardinality. The vector-selection row lists
three ANN strategies side by side: HNSW (sub-linear,
approximate), IVF (sub-linear in the probe set,
approximate), and brute-force scan (linear, exact). The
CBO picks among them by combining the four cost
components; an ANN strategy is only chosen when its
approximation cost fits the application's `ρ_min`
constraint.

**Total cost.** For a plan tree `T = op(T₁, ..., Tₖ)`,

$$
\mathrm{Cost}(T) \;=\; \mathrm{Cost}_{\mathrm{op}}(T) \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathrm{Cost}(T_i) \;+\; \mathrm{io}_{\mathrm{materialize}}(T),
$$

where `io_materialize` is the cost of materializing
intermediate results at materialization points (the
spill cost, which is incurred only when a subtree is
forced to materialize by, e.g., a non-pipelineable
join). The cost is recursive: the cost of a plan is
the sum of its operators' costs, each evaluated at the
input cardinality determined by the plan's structure.

**CBO complexity.** The CBO's search space is

$$
|\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{CBO}}(Q)| \;\le\; |\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{RBO}}(Q)| \cdot |\mathrm{Phys}|^n,
$$

where `|Phys|` is the number of physical
implementations of each logical operator and `n` is the
number of operator occurrences in the canonical form
of `Q`. The exponential factor in `n` comes from the
choice of physical implementation at each operator; the
factor `|Space_RBO|` comes from the RBO's plan space
(6.5). The CBO's worst-case runtime is

$$
T_{\mathrm{CBO}} \;\le\; |\mathrm{Space}_{\mathrm{RBO}}(Q)| \cdot |\mathrm{Phys}|^n \cdot t_{\mathrm{cost}},
$$

where `t_{\mathrm{cost}}` is the time to evaluate the
cost function for one physical plan.

**Pruning heuristics.** The CBO applies three
standard pruning techniques, all compatible with
correctness:

- **Dynamic programming over join orderings** (Selinger-
  style). For the `⋈`-fragment of the algebra, the CBO
  computes the optimal left-deep tree in `O(n^2)` time
  using the recurrence of [Selinger et al., 1979].
- **Greedy selection for vector predicates.** When the
  vector selections are estimated to be highly
  selective, the CBO evaluates them in selectivity
  order (most selective first), without exploring
  all `k!` orderings.
- **Top-K pruning for `ORDER BY`.** When `τ` is
  followed by `Λ_K`, the CBO prunes physical plans
  whose cost exceeds the best top-K cost found so far
  (per T1).

**Open problem.** When is cost-based optimization in
the new algebra tractable? The classical join-ordering
problem is solvable in polynomial time for left-deep
trees (Selinger) but NP-hard for bushy trees [Ibaraki
& Kameda, 1984]. With vector selections as first-class
operators (each contributing an `ef`-parameterized
physical realization), graph expansions (each
contributing a direction-parameterized physical
realization), and materializations (each potentially
shared or distinct), the CBO's search space is richer
than the classical join-ordering problem. Identifying
polynomial subclasses — for example, "queries with at
most one graph expansion and at most two materializations
are optimizable in polynomial time" — is an open
problem to which the algebra of this paper provides
the formal vocabulary.

