## 9. Related Theoretical Work

The MosaicOpt algebra sits at the intersection of
classical relational algebra, algebraic extensions
of the relational model, the theory of equational
rewriting, and the cost-based optimization literature.
This section surveys the most directly relevant prior
work in each of these threads and locates MosaicOpt's
contribution precisely. The goal is not to enumerate
all related work but to identify the *lineage* of the
algebra and to make clear what is novel.

### 9.1 Classical relational algebra

The lineage of MosaicOpt begins with Codd's
relational algebra [Codd, 1970], which showed that a
small set of operators — selection, projection,
Cartesian product, union, set difference, and rename
— is sufficient to express any first-order query.
Subsequent work refined the operator set (adding
explicit `⋈` for theta-join, grouping/aggregation,
ordering, and limits) and crystallized in SQL. Date's
textbook synthesis [Date, 2003] and Ullman's
[Ullman, 1988] established the relational algebra as
the *lingua franca* of database theory.

The second foundational pillar is **query
optimization**. Selinger's cost-based optimizer for
System R [Selinger et al., 1979] established the
template that nearly every commercial RDBMS still
follows: enumerate alternative orderings and access
paths, estimate cost under a cardinality model, pick
the cheapest. The **Volcano extensible query
evaluation framework** [Graefe, 1994] and the
**Cascades** optimizer [Graefe, 1995] generalized
this to a *rule-based* outer loop driving a
*cost-based* inner search, in which equivalence rules
over logical operators are first-class objects, and
the optimizer is itself extensible by adding rules.

The insight of Cascades — that the algebra is the API
— is the direct ancestor of MosaicOpt's design. A
new operator is shipped with its equivalence rules and
cost model, and the optimizer gains new capabilities
without any central rewrite. MosaicOpt inherits this
property.

### 9.2 Algebraic extensions of the relational model

MosaicOpt is not the first proposal to extend the
relational algebra. The literature is rich; we
highlight the most directly relevant threads.

- **Nested relational algebra.** The nested
  relational algebra [Thomas & Fischer, 1986]
  extends Codd's algebra with set-valued attributes,
  enabling expression of object-oriented queries
  without a join. MosaicOpt's first-class
  materialization is in the spirit of the nested
  algebra's power to compute and bind complex
  values, but the focus is different: nested algebra
  is about *structuring* complex values, while
  MosaicOpt is about *binding* computed values
  (vector distances, BM25 scores) for downstream
  use.

- **Object-relational algebra.** Beech's extension
  [Beech, 1989] and the SQL-99 object-relational
  model added user-defined types and methods to the
  relational substrate. MosaicOpt's typed
  vector/text/graph columns are in the same
  spirit — a "richer type system on top of the
  relational model" — but the algebraic treatment
  is more disciplined: a typed column is a
  *first-class predicate* and a *first-class
  materialization expression*, not just a method
  call.

- **Temporal algebra.** Tuzhilin and Clifford's
  [1990] temporal algebra and Snodgrass's
  [Snodgrass, 1995] temporal extensions add
  time-varying attributes and time-aware predicates
  to the relational model. MosaicOpt's
  graph-expansion operator `Γ` is in the same
  spirit — extending the model to a dimension
  (graph) that classical algebra does not capture
  — but the dimensions are different. The
  equational theory of `Γ` (G1) is the analogue of
  the temporal-algebra identities.

- **Semi-structured and XML algebra.** The
  semi-structured algebra [Fernandez, Simeon &
  Wadler, 2000] and the various XPath/XQuery
  algebras add a *tree* data model. The
  structural recursion of these algebras is
  similar in spirit to MosaicOpt's `Γ` (which
  recurses over a graph relation), but the
  equational theory is different: the
  semi-structured algebras emphasize *bisimulation*
  and *structural equivalence*, while MosaicOpt
  emphasizes *selection pushdown through graph
  expansion* (G1).

These extensions share MosaicOpt's pattern of
"extend the algebra, prove identities". MosaicOpt's
contribution is to extend in a *new direction*
(multi-modal: text + vector + graph, in the
context of agentic memory) and to provide, in one
place, a *SQL surface* and an *equational theory*
that the prior extensions did not.

### 9.3 Equational theories in query optimization

The classical identities of relational algebra —
selection pushdown (A4), join commutativity, projection
pushdown (A5) — have been part of the optimizer's
arsenal since System R. What MosaicOpt adds is (i) a
*new set of identities* (M1–M3, V1, J1, T1, G1) for
the extended operators, and (ii) an explicit
*convergent-rewriting* analysis (4.6) that proves the
extended rewriting system is terminating and confluent.

The convergent-rewriting analysis draws on a
classical literature that pre-dates databases:

- **Newman's lemma** [Newman, 1942]: termination +
  local confluence ⟹ confluence. This is the
  formal foundation of the rewriting-system
  analysis in 4.6.
- **Huet's work on confluence** [Huet, 1980]:
  critical-pair analysis as a sufficient condition
  for local confluence. We use critical-pair
  analysis to enumerate the overlapping-redex cases
  in 4.6.
- **The Terese textbook** [Terese, 2003]: a
  comprehensive treatment of abstract rewriting
  systems. The orientation of the equational
  theory into a rewriting system (4.6) follows the
  textbook's pattern of *orienting an equational
  theory into a reduction system and proving its
  confluence*.

In the database literature, the closest prior work
is Atzeni and Torlone's [Atzeni & Torlone, 1996]
treatment of view maintenance as algebraic rewriting.
Their setting is different (view maintenance vs.
query optimization), but the *pattern* is the same:
orient an equational theory, prove confluence, and
exploit the normal form to drive a transformation.

MosaicOpt's convergent-rewriting analysis is, to our
knowledge, the first such analysis for a relational
algebra that includes vector, text, and graph
operators.

### 9.4 Cost-based optimization complexity

The complexity of cost-based optimization is a
classical subject. Selinger et al. [1979] gave an
`O(n^2)` dynamic programming algorithm for left-deep
join orderings. Ibaraki and Kameda [1984] showed that
the join-ordering problem for *bushy* trees is
NP-hard. Pellenkoft, Galindo-Legaria, and Kersten
[Pellenkoft et al., 1996] studied the complexity of
join ordering with outer joins and other extensions.

MosaicOpt's CBO (7.5) inherits this complexity. The
join-ordering subproblem is identical to the
classical one; the additions are (i) vector
selections as first-class operators (each
contributing an `ef`-parameterized physical
realization), (ii) graph expansions (each
contributing a direction-parameterized realization),
and (iii) materializations (each potentially shared
or distinct). The CBO's search space is therefore
richer than the classical one; identifying
polynomial subclasses is an open problem (10.1).

More recent work on cost-based optimization for
hybrid systems includes the learned optimizer
literature (Balsa, CostFormer) and the cardinality
estimation literature. These are *physical* and
*empirical* contributions — not theoretical
contributions to the equational theory of the
algebra — and are orthogonal to MosaicOpt.

### 9.5 Hybrid query algebra (recent)

The last decade has seen a sustained effort to bring
vector and relational together, but with very few
*theoretical* contributions. The systems are
numerous; the algebras are few.

- **Vector-aware RDBMS extensions** —
  PostgreSQL's `pgvector`, DuckDB's `vss`, Oracle
  AI Vector Search — add a vector type, an ANN
  index, and vector distance predicates.
  Integration is at the function-call level. There
  are no first-class identities for vector
  selection pushdown, no cost model for
  approximation, no principled way to rewrite a
  hybrid query. These are *systems*, not
  *theories*.

- **Purpose-built hybrid stores** — Pinecone,
  Weaviate, Qdrant, Milvus, Vespa — are
  vector-first, with metadata filtering bolted on.
  Their optimization story is pragmatic, not
  foundational. Their planners have rewrites, but
  the rewrites are not exposed as named identities.

- **Multi-modal query languages** — SQL/MM, SPARQL
  with full-text extensions, Cypher, and various
  research prototypes — mix graph, text, and (in
  a few cases) vector. SPARQL has a careful
  algebraic foundation, but its fusion with vector
  predicates is still an open area. Cypher is
  graph-first; SQL/MM is multi-modal but does not
  address vector similarity or ANN.

- **The MosaicOpt project** — is, to our knowledge,
  the first to provide a *unified algebraic
  foundation* for text, vector, and graph in the
  context of agentic memory, with a *proven
  equational theory* (4.6, Theorem 4.2) and a
  *formal cost model* (7.5).

The common pattern in the prior systems: every
system mixes the modalities by *fusing scores at
the application layer*, not by *extending the
algebra that defines what counts as an answer*.
MosaicOpt's contribution is to make the fusion
*first-class in the algebra* — a materialization
expression is a syntactic construct, the optimizer
sees it, the cost model accounts for it, and the
equational theory of 4 applies to it.

### 9.6 What this paper does not cover

Three conscious exclusions bound the scope of the
contribution.

- **We do not propose a new physical data
  structure.** HNSW, B-trees, and inverted indexes
  are taken as external; the paper's contribution
  is the *algebraic* use of these primitives, not
  their internal design. A learned-index primitive
  [Kraska et al., 2018] would be a drop-in
  replacement in the bridge table of 8.2; the
  algebra is unchanged.

- **We do not propose a new learning-based
  optimization.** CostFormer, Balsa, and the
  learned-cardinality literature are *empirical*
  contributions to the *physical* layer. The
  MosaicOpt contribution is at the *logical* layer:
  the algebra, its equational theory, and the
  cost-model *framework*. A learned optimizer can
  be plugged in to *instantiate* the cost model of
  7.5; the algebra and equational theory are
  unchanged.

- **We do not propose a new transaction model.**
  The algebra is read-only. Transactional
  extensions (writes, isolation levels,
  multi-version concurrency) are out of scope and
  are addressed in 10.5.

These exclusions are not weaknesses; they are
*scope choices* that keep the contribution focused
on the algebraic foundation. The algebra is the
paper's central contribution, and the paper
argues that the algebra, by itself, is a useful
and complete theoretical contribution.

